WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.067
Low-impact cloud services are generally associated with limited adverse effects from a security incident.

00:00:05.067 --> 00:00:07.905
That does not mean the service is unimportant,

00:00:07.905 --> 00:00:11.453
and it definitely does not mean security can be ignored.

00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:15.355
It means the expected harm from a loss of confidentiality, integrity,

00:00:15.355 --> 00:00:20.322
or availability is lower than it would be for more sensitive or mission-critical systems.

00:00:20.322 --> 00:00:23.515
The baseline should still drive disciplined implementation and evidence.

00:00:23.515 --> 00:00:25.998
Low-risk SaaS concepts, including tailored low-impact paths,

00:00:25.998 --> 00:00:29.901
are meant to recognize that some software services have limited sensitivity,

00:00:29.901 --> 00:00:32.384
limited complexity, and a narrower security footprint.

00:00:32.384 --> 00:00:36.641
A focused path can make sense when the service is genuinely low

00:00:36.641 --> 00:00:40.898
impact and the operating model is simple enough to support reduced assessment

00:00:40.898 --> 00:00:41.548
complexity.

00:00:41.548 --> 00:00:47.225
That is different from pretending a complicated or sensitive system is low impact to avoid work.

00:00:47.225 --> 00:00:49.708
Even in lower-impact scenarios, common responsibilities remain.

00:00:49.708 --> 00:00:51.482
Access control should be understandable.

00:00:51.482 --> 00:00:56.094
Logging should support accountability and investigation at the level appropriate for the service.

00:00:56.094 --> 00:00:57.868
Vulnerability management should be active.

00:00:57.868 --> 00:00:59.287
Configuration should be managed.

00:00:59.287 --> 00:01:01.061
Incident response should be defined.

00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:02.835
Continuous monitoring should not disappear;

00:01:02.835 --> 00:01:06.382
it should be scaled to the system context and risk.

00:01:06.382 --> 00:01:09.220
Low impact still requires a real security program.

00:01:09.220 --> 00:01:12.059
For small SaaS providers entering the federal market,

00:01:12.059 --> 00:01:15.252
the low-impact conversation can be a useful starting point.

00:01:15.252 --> 00:01:19.864
It encourages the team to define the boundary, identify what data is handled,

00:01:19.864 --> 00:01:21.637
clarify customer and provider responsibilities,

00:01:21.637 --> 00:01:25.540
and prove that the service is operated in a controlled way.

00:01:25.540 --> 00:01:30.507
The best lower-impact packages are not thin packages; they are focused packages where scope,

00:01:30.507 --> 00:01:32.340
evidence, and risk are clearly aligned.
